`CompanionExecution` binds a checked companion map to its original parameter and parameter-error readings before numerical access. `Heap::evaluate_companion` imports the exact rational coefficients once, then evaluates the full radius or two-sided covariance map with outward error propagation. Its complete immutable result retains the source parameter identity and every ordered target binding; reads refuse after either parameter channel or either target changes version, including equal-value writes. `CompanionAdapter::entries()` continues to denote stored output parameters and is never reused as the source of this transformation. This read-only parameter evaluation establishes neither positive semidefiniteness nor a statistical model and does not publish a new heap bundle.

`PhasorOperand` binds one complete Complex block to all four identity scalar views admitted in the same atomic input family. `PhasorProduct` matches both ordered operands against `PhasorBlockCorrespondence` before numerical access. `Heap::evaluate_phasor_product` contracts the actual stored copies with outward arithmetic, retaining separate deterministic totals and numerical errors without double counting; differing repeated and negated copies keep their own declared errors. The complete immutable result retains the native original product and every source/companion version. The immutable evaluation alone does not initialize heap readings or establish a solve, residual transfer or statistical model.

Statistical covariance and its numerical evaluation error are separate from deterministic radii. Repeated operands retain dependence; absent or unknown correlations never mean independence. Affine moments and nonlinear local linearizations retain different assurance. A budget refusal can preserve a valid certificate whose bound is insufficient; a small residual alone cannot pass the budget. Factor reuse revalidates exact operator dependencies and recomputes each new result's certificate.

Successful runs of the same exact target reproduce within tracked error under admitted arithmetic models. Centers may differ across valid schedules or environments. The existing same-environment transcript tests are narrower regression evidence, not a cross-machine bitwise guarantee.

## Current scope and limits · `sec:matrix:scope`

Implemented operations include copy, homogeneous affine/precision conversion, addition/subtraction, fixed linear maps, entry transforms, matrix products, elementwise square/reciprocal/square root, verified nonempty square solve/inverse, finite recovery, eligible factor reuse and the documented statistical propagation. Original physical and whole-box domain checks remain mandatory. Numerical π-dependent conversion refuses at exact export; the current kernels do not include a certified π evaluator, generic eigensolver or integrator.

Binding admission in `src/binding/symbols.rs` requires complete coverage and refuses unrelated overlapping declarations. Homogeneous recipes remain restricted to coherent concrete differences. `select_reading` admits coherent identity-coordinate homogeneous general readings with scalar or column identity-map deterministic-bound and numerical-error companions. It prepares the selected symbols on an explicit caller ledger, retains them with the alias, and leaves admission costs unchanged for paths that never select those symbols. Each companion uses its own ordered storage map and exact target, including shared parameter regions. Input snapshots and operation dependencies expose the original companion definitions and every retained copy discrepancy. Covariance, nonidentity companion maps and native members remain explicit facade refusals. `replace_reading_companion_entries` validates finite nonnegative radii and error-within-total before atomic replacement; even an equal-bit write invalidates all affected selected sources and downstream outputs. General expanded layouts admit complete checked Complex blocks. Differing repeated or negative centers require distinct numerical-error companions targeting both actual scalar copies, with identity parameter maps. An outward discrepancy check retains `CopyDiscrepancy` records naming both stored positions, both selected error parameters and the exact expanded family; missing coverage or an unproved discrepancy refuses atomically. Every later reader rechecks both error-parameter versions. These are declared errors about one exact lineage, with no independence inference; general companion-map execution remains separate. The public lifecycle in `tests/integration/complex_copies.rs` requests `DependencyPolicy::WholeBase` explicitly; a complete expanded write already invalidates the family conservatively. An `Entries` request retains its exact cumulative resource refusal instead of silently substituting another dependency policy. Positive-reciprocal kernel scratch has the scoped ticket inventory described above; the separate deterministic solve inventory covers RHS, certificate, restoration and recovery arrays. The solve-owned sensitivity and moment arrays and the native LC phasor arrays also have scoped inventories. The general-statistics inventory covers pre-binding parameter capture, generic statistical executors and standalone cross-covariance; other private kernels retain separate obligations.

Cached CAS reading and witness dependency inventories are reused on writes. Direct family maps retain per-entry revisions: writing the current component invalidates that component and the whole state while an independent voltage component survives. Domain predicates and overlap proofs are queried separately, including across reading groups on the same graph. Missing fine inventories and unsupported storage-to-family maps invalidate conservatively. Ordinary whole-base replacement and retirement also invalidate readers. Native bounded results refuse value-only entry writes. `Heap::replace_reading_bundle` replaces selected centers, deterministic totals and numerical errors in one prepared transaction, retaining untouched entries and advancing all native target and companion revisions together. It invalidates every old native result reading and dependent numerical result, even for unchanged centers. Replacement radii are explicit declarations for the new version; no earlier computation certificate survives. Incomplete or nonfinite bundles, negative radii, errors exceeding totals, expanded families, general companion maps and product-contribution bundles refuse before publication. The caller’s cumulative Work and staged-payload policy covers the replacement schedule. The two-entry native fixture’s selected bundle replacement costs 257 Work and 1,392 staged bytes, with exact `N`/`N−1` and cumulative precharge checks; existing numerical kernel pins remain unchanged. The public voltage lifecycle in `tests/integration/bounded_readings.rs` checks physical target preservation and stale descendants.

## Testing · `sec:matrix:testing`

Public conformance tests check exact-rational containment, independent square-root/norm oracles, strict domains, stale/foreign handles, alias selections, publication rollback, numerical coverage and exact `N`/`N−1` capacity/work boundaries in both formats. Compile twins enforce category, precision, borrow and checked-state boundaries. Symbol lifecycle tests cover pending/abandoned outputs, wrong owners, complete companion inventories and repeated copies.

Integration acceptance exercises the physical truss and reduced common-source resistor ladder, restored outputs and cross-point covariance. The full five-by-five statistical ladder remains a symbolic-budget refusal; direct mixed numerical admission also refuses. These tests do not stand in for LC execution. Run `cargo test -p nrz-matrix --test public` for public contracts and lifecycles. `docs/records/matrix-core-acceptance.md` records the acceptance environment, measured costs and limits; `src/solve/mod.rs` retains the measurement commands and coverage details.

`PhasorPublication` fixes a fresh complete output family before numerical execution: one whole phasor, its two-coordinate vector, all four scalar copies and separate identity total/error companions. `Heap::publish_phasor_product` initializes those views and both radius regions in one atomic heap commit, preserving independently rounded copies. Every output retains `PhasorResultDefinition`, the original operand assurance scopes and a flattened closure of the original selected sources; stale operands or parameters invalidate output access. Generated totals preserve represented-input assurance and cannot promote it to declared physical bounds. Reusing an already bound output root, omitting the vector/copy inventory, exhausting resources or failing allocation publishes no partial family. Shared-voltage statistical result publication remains a separate contract.

`PhasorSolve` binds the original LC equation to every impedance and voltage copy before numerical access. `Heap::evaluate_phasor_solve` lowers the exact references and fixed input components, checks each fixed copy against its own numerical error, refuses zero or zero-containing reactance, and reuses the existing Real inverse-defect, residual and common-operator perturbation verifier. Its numerical-only radius includes every original copy discrepancy; the deterministic total already includes that radius. Coherent voltage/current coordinates restore identically. `PhasorSolvePublication` requires the whole current phasor, its component vector, all four scalar copies and all eight total/error parameters before execution; one heap transaction publishes them together with the flattened original source closure. A source or companion mutation invalidates all affected restored readings. The exact norm/reference transfer remains distinct from the verifier’s residual over independently rounded block copies. No phase-angle or spectral claim is supplied. The complete solve/restoration operation ledger costs 1,842,545 Work; exact reference/fixed-component lowering costs 336 Work, numerical certification 2,600, and current storage 5,520. Current and restored-voltage publication plans retain 95,335 and 95,499 bytes, respectively; solve evaluation peaks at 756,904 staged/output bytes.

`PhasorSolveEvaluation::inventory` retains the native solve's original equation, physical reference definitions, selected source copies and own-target companion channels. A finite plan precedes reference and fixed-input enclosures, scalar input-copy vectors, both complete input blocks, inverse/current candidates, all six shared-verifier vectors, intermediate proof fields, final current/total/error arrays, final certificate and metric-transfer fields, reconstructed residual/current bounds and both residual-norm input pairs. Non-cloneable permits remain attached to actual numerical owners. Publication authenticates the complete final owners and all three `ReadingRegion` copy permits before committing any heap reading. Evaluation clones share every retained numerical owner, while historical receipts retain definitions and capacity observations without keeping those numerical owners alive. Fixed arrays report their exact inline size and vectors report actual capacity; peak payload follows the common live schedule. The existing 4096-byte native retained allowance covers the new aligned metadata and planned numerical owners. Numerical certification remains 2600 Work and exact lowering remains 336 Work. Heap destination slabs, the restoration product, other native recipes and execution-wide retirement retain separate obligations; no faer storage allowance applies to the native solver.

The complete native current publication has 49 observed owners and 152 primitive floats, totaling 608/1216 numerical bytes in binary32/binary64. Its 34 vectors hold exactly 64 primitive floats (256/512 bytes); fixed fields hold the other 88. Compact fixed-input storage omits absent components and scalar companion reads reserve their exact lengths. Common live payload peaks at 336/672 bytes. The aligned original-plan allocation is 496 bytes, and new retained metadata totals 604/600 bytes, including the 24-byte inline receipt and the shared final owner metadata. The four-byte binary32 difference is alignment padding around its certificate owner. Both metadata ceilings have exact success/one-below boundaries. These scoped figures exclude heap destination slabs, compiler stack-frame layout, allocator overhead and shared original exact declarations. `examples/phasor_inventory_measurements.rs` separates evaluation and publication clocks from fixture construction and lists every numerical owner.

The fixed-reactance fixture binds the decoder’s own literal-import displacement to each nonzero stored copy’s numerical-error companion; this error has no physical or statistical meaning, and exact zero components remain exact.

`Heap::evaluate_phasor_statistics` evaluates a complete shared-voltage family through three original-source `CompanionExecution` plans: left variance, signed cross-covariance and right variance. Both means must be scalar readings from the exact certified solves retained by the affine covariance laws. Every block must use the same original parameter and numerical-error bindings. The immutable result exposes both means, all three moments and their separate numerical errors only while all source and target versions remain current; any failure returns no statistical prefix. Evaluated arrays are the boundary because the source declares finite moments without a positive-semidefiniteness or model-admission certificate. The complete evaluated family preserves the existing finite-moment executor contract and its dependency checks without introducing a new heap statistical-admission contract. Deterministic current and voltage families retain their complete atomic heap publications. The shared-voltage operation ledger costs 2,609,278 Work, including 17,092 for both mean publications, 2,655 for companion planning, 444 for covariance lowering and 39 for numerical evaluation. The complete statistical family retains 426,761 bytes and peaks at 776,292 staged bytes.

Output payload is charged by owned allocation: a publication references its already charged shared solve or product plan, retains the actual encoded witnesses, and adds only its own metadata and source records. Shared phasor equation definitions count once per allocation across the statistical family; independently allocated equations remain distinct. The restoration product shares its original correspondence, result definition and operand groups; its new plan and evaluation charge their own records and arrays. The default one MiB output ceiling is unchanged.

The full five-by-five ladder sensitivity passes draft validation under the integrated default policy, then refuses witness replay at 2,000,003 required `ReplayWork` against the unchanged 2,000,000 ceiling. `Binding::validate` currently supplies that policy internally; `ExecutionLimits` has no symbolic-work field. Explicit construction/generation and replay boundaries are tested through `Context::check_solve_sensitivity` and `LimitsOverride`, with all 35 original operator, load and solution symbols retained. A future representation saving may make the integrated case succeed; that requires changing the refusal test to a success test rather than preserving a historical failure.
